      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "We compared the effectiveness and safety of endotracheal intubation using the GlideScope (GS) video laryngoscope, CTrach laryngeal mask airway (LMA), or Shikani optical stylet rigid laryngoscope (SOS) during elective cervical surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Forty-five patients undergoing elective cervical surgery were randomly and equally assigned to endotracheal intubation via GS, LMA, or SOS airway management."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Endotracheal intubation was successfully completed in all patients. The mean intubation times of the groups differed significantly (P\u200a<\u200a.01): GS, 17.9\u200a±\u200a3.1\u200as; SOS, 40.4\u200a±\u200a13.7\u200as; and LMA, 80.5\u200a±\u200a22.5\u200as. The groups had similar heart rates and mean arterial pressures throughout the intubation, except that at 2\u200aminutes after intubation the mean arterial pressure of the GS group (106.1\u200a±\u200a18.5\u200amm Hg) was significantly higher than that of the LMA (89.7\u200a±\u200a18.5\u200amm Hg) or SOS (89.7\u200a±\u200a18.5\u200amm Hg; P\u200a<\u200a.01). The change in C2-5 Cobb angle from baseline was significantly higher in the GS group (GS, 34.2°\u200a±\u200a7.3°) than the LMA (24.4°\u200a±\u200a5.8°) or SOS (25.5°\u200a±\u200a6.4°); P\u200a<\u200a.01)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The CTrach LMA and SOS rigid laryngoscope are effective, safe alternatives to the GS video laryngoscope for patients undergoing elective cervical surgery."
        }
      ]},
